Structure of shell

Parser -> Executor -> Subsystems

Parser = reads command line and inputes into data structure "Command Table"
Executor = take table and create process for each simple command, create pipes for output of processes, and redirect std ioe if any.
Subsys = ENV, wildcards, subshells

Data Structure for command table:

typedef struct SimCom
{
	int		nAvailableArguments;
	int		nArguments;
	char	**arguments;
}	
typedef struct CommandTable
{
	int		nSimpleCommands;
	SimCom	**simcom;
	char	*outfile;
	char	*infile;
	char	*errfile;
	int		background;
}

Lexical Analyzer:
Separates input into tokens.

printf, malloc, free, write, access, open, read,
close, fork, wait, waitpid, wait3, wait4, signal,
sigaction, sigemptyset, sigaddset, kill, exit,
getcwd, chdir, stat, lstat, fstat, unlink, execve,
dup, dup2, pipe, opendir, readdir, closedir,
strerror, perror, isatty, ttyname, ttyslot, ioctl,
getenv, tcsetattr, tcgetattr, tgetent, tgetflag,
tgetnum, tgetstr, tgoto, tputs

Allowed functions:
readline - reads single line from file opened in read mode, up to \n or n bytes
char	*readline(const char *prompt);
rl_clear_history - clear history list, different from clear_history
rl_on_new_line - tells update fns to move onto new (empty) line, usu after \n.
rl_replace_line - replace contents of rl_line_buffer w text.
rl_redisplay - change what's displayed on screen to reflect rl_line_buffer.
add_history - 

wait/ waitpid - accepts pointer to an int of exit status of child process.
wait3/ wait 4 - accepts addn args, flags specifying wait behaviour, rusage

